    },
    section {
        head {
            0,
            "Conclusion",
        },
        par {
            'The main points I took from this text are:',
        },
        par {
            list {
                il,
                par {
                    'You may not be able to control the outside world, but you can control your inside world.',
                },
                par {
                    'What is good and bad is a matter of opinion and can be changed.',
                },
                par {
                    'You must maintain your good nature by doing good.',
                },
                par {
                    'You must be good to others and your environment.',
                },
                par {
                    'Change is constant and natural and you are part of the ever morphing mass that is the world.',
                },
                par {
                    'Do not suffer for that which is not worth suffering for.',
                },
                par {
                    'Do not despair about the natural goings of the world.',
                },
            },
            'Themes seem to be change, death, suffering, virtue and social dealings / humanity.',
        },
        par {
            'I don’t think that this is a book of mind bending positions or radical thoughts.
Neither of grand narratives or deep theories.
But it presents many good reminders.
Most of the time when I could make sense of the writing, it seemed quite solid.
All put together it certainly has a style, a cohesive vibe.
I think it is a book that is worth reading, and one that can influence you positively.
Many of the themes and points he makes are still very relevant and applicable to our contemporary lives.
Of course there are some critiques I make here and there.
And I do not understand everything that is presented.
Following are two gripes and confusions I have with this work:',
        },
        section {
            head {
                0,
                "Evil",
            },
            par {
                'Many times he says that what seems negative to us is a matter of our own opinion,
and that opinion can be changed.
And that this opinion is what makes us suffer, not the “bad” event itself.
However, he also seems to speak of evil, true evil even (',
                link {
                    "#ix:xiv",
                    "IX:XIV",
                    tags {
                        "local",
                    },
                },
                ').
Is this evil then not also a figment of our opinion?
And if there is true evil, what makes this evil so real and bad,
and all those bad things in our head only bad through our misguided opinions?
This is very confusing to me.',
            },
        },
        section {
            head {
                0,
                "Feelings",
            },
            par {
                'It seems to be a common theme that the way of dealing with negative feelings is to find an
explanation as to why it is not so bad.
Usually of the flavour that an event is only natural with the focus on accepting worldly changes.
I would not reduce stoicism to “not so bad, don’t be sad” but sometimes Aurelius does
come close to that at times.
With all the accepting of what happens in the outer world, he doesn’t seem to put the same emphasis
on accepting what happens in the inner world.
I’m still not sure if he thinks of emotions that have come up as events in a world as well,
or if the spirit is different in this regard.
Since you often cannot change what happens in the world, focusing on accepting future events
makes sense.
Since you do have more control over what you think (and feel to some extend),
it makes sense that he focuses more on changing future experience.
However I think a little attention to accepting negative experiences would be good as well.
Of course, with this position I might be going against one of stoicisms premier principles.
Furthermore, he very much seems to like reducing negative experiences and not
increasing positive experiences.
To me this seems similar to how Buddhist think about it.
I’m not convinced yet that it is best to only do away with suffering and not to seek any pleasure.
I don’t think Aurelius says such a thing directly but he seems to think in that direction.',
            },
        },
    },
},
